WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.260
Pull up a chair. This is Models and Agents, episode 125 on July 28, 2026.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:10.000
Let's see what shipped. The AI world never sleeps.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.180
Here's what you need to know today.

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:19.280
MCP's largest update removes session stickiness so agents can scale behind ordinary load balancers.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:25.380
The model context protocol now runs fully stateless with mandatory issuer validation.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:30.520
It adds official extensions for interactive interfaces and long running tasks.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:35.220
Snowflake introduced a new gateway for enterprise control over agents.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:39.860
Microsoft released a 5 billion active parameter cyber defense model,

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:44.300
plus a .NET library that pulls skills from MCP servers.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:49.440
The model context protocol just received its biggest architectural change since launch.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:52.680
The protocol moved to a fully stateless design

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:56.100
and strengthened authentication to block mix-up attacks.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:01.400
MCP apps and MCP tasks graduated to official extensions.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.500
Servers can now sit behind standard load balancers without sticky sessions.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:11.000
Clients received durable task handles that survive a single connection.

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:15.400
Payload sizes increased only modestly and remain compressible.

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:20.120
Out-of-band logging was removed after usage data showed almost none.

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:27.040
A 12-month deprecation policy and an enterprise-managed authorization extension developed with OCTA

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:30.300
give larger organizations the stability they requested.

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:35.700
SDK downloads now reach roughly 250 million per week.

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:39.780
The Agentic AI Foundation has 240 members.

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:45.920
The update finalizes support for more than 100 MCP servers in the new transport layer

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:49.260
and adds multi-round trip request negotiation.

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:53.760
Builders running production agents should test the new transport layer

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:58.640
and the tasks extension for any workflow that once needed persistent connections.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:04.000
Microsoft released MAI-Cyber-1-Flash.

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:08.140
The model contains 137 billion total parameters

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:11.980
with 5 billion active sparse mixture of experts parameters.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:20.020
It is a fine tune of MAI-Code-1-Flash and carries a 256,000 token context window.

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:26.640
The model runs inside the M-Dash Agentic scanning harness where it handles up to 90% of tasks.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:32.260
That lifts the overall system to 95.95% on CyberGym.

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:40.060
It is not offered as a standalone endpoint and targets cyber defense workloads inside Microsoft's multi-model pipeline.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:45.400
A new tutorial shows how to run the one-bit bonsai 27 billion model.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:58.640
The setup uses the PrismML fork of llama.cpp and it specialized CUDA kernels for the Q1 underscore 0 underscore G128 GGUF format.

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:03.980
The endpoint exposes an open AI compatible interface for local inference.

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:11.300
The quantization format requires those PrismML kernels to decode correctly on consumer GPUs.

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:19.820
Developers working with extreme quantization on consumer GPUs now have a documented path that preserves usable output quality.

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:27.920
The tutorial demonstrates inference on a single consumer GPU while maintaining the open AI compatible endpoint.

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:33.180
Andthropic published its formal position on open hyphen weights models.

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:39.480
The document covers safety, proliferation, and commercial use without releasing any new model.

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:46.100
It addresses licensing, safety commitments, and differences in security practices across the industry.

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:53.300
The post clarifies the company's views on safety, proliferation, and commercial use after weeks of speculation.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:04:02.360
Teams deciding between closed frontier APIs and open weight deployments should read the full document for the lab's explicit risk assessment.

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:11.160
The document addresses licensing, safety commitments, and the divergence between open weight support and security practices across the industry.

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:20.960
Snowflake launched Cortex AI Gateway, the control plane enforces identity, policy, and audit for agents that touch enterprise data.

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:30.320
It supports more than 100 MCP servers and provides dual attribution that records both agent identity and the human delegator.

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:33.360
Spend limits help prevent runaway costs.

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:40.400
The Gateway builds on the May 2026 Natoma acquisition and integrates with one password,

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:45.360
Sailpoint, Saviant, AMBIT, and Link security in private preview.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:49.900
Tasks scoped access tokens replace inherited user permissions.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:54.620
Runtime monitoring compares every action against the original human intent.

00:04:55.440 --> 00:05:04.720
Times released version 3B, the update adds centralized controls for automation builders who need policy enforcement across multiple agent platforms.

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:13.760
Security and operations teams already using tines for no code workflows can now apply the same governance layer to autonomous agents.

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:21.680
The release targets organizations that must audit and constrain agent actions across heterogeneous tool ecosystems.

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:29.280
Tines released version 3B focused on governing AI workflows, apps and agents at enterprise scale.

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:36.680
Semantic kernel now lets agents discover and load skills directly from any MCP server at runtime.

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:42.620
A central team can publish skills once and agents pull only what they need per deployment.

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:49.180
The change removes the need to copy skill folders into every container image or binary distribution.

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:56.860
Semantic kernel now lets agents discover and load skills directly from any MCP server at runtime,

00:05:56.860 --> 00:05:59.860
instead of embedding them in the application binary.

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:07.900
.NET developers building agent applications should test the new discovery flow on existing MCP servers.

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:16.880
A new policy improvement reinforcement learning framework adds a retrospective verification step after each base algorithm update.

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:28.400
The authors released code at the repository mentioned in the r-slash machine learning post and report gains across math, code, and tool use tasks plus better training stability.

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:35.060
The method works as a plug-and-play layer that does not replace the base algorithm's local credit assignment.

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:43.200
If the updated policy improves on a sliding historical window, it reinforces the change, otherwise it corrects the direction.

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:57.320
The authors released code at github.com slash JCE Kent or May slash PEO underscore Viral and report gains across math, code, and tool use tasks plus better training stability.

00:06:58.100 --> 00:07:03.740
RL practitioners should try PEO as a plug-and-layer on top of their current post-training loop.

00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:11.340
GPT Live in chat GPT voice is now rolled out to education, business, and enterprise plans worldwide.

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:17.660
The feature gives teams real-time voice interaction with the model without consumer plan restrictions.

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:25.060
Organizations already on those plans can enable it immediately for internal tooling and customer facing prototypes.

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:33.140
The rollout removes previous geographic and plan-based gating that had limited the voice mode to individual consumer accounts.

00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:37.720
Okay, let's pop the hood on closed loop policy improvement.

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:44.800
Everyone talks about reinforcement learning post-training as a single forward pass that just gets better each step.

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:53.220
In practice, the update can degrade performance because of sampling noise, imperfect credit assignment, and stochastic generation.

00:07:54.160 --> 00:08:02.680
PIRL adds a second verification phase after the base optimizer, such as PEO or GRPO, takes its exploratory step.

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:09.000
The next iteration measures whether the new policy actually outperforms a sliding historical anchor.

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:13.060
If the measured gain is positive, the direction is reinforced.

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:16.300
If negative, the update is suppressed or reversed.

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:22.180
This adds one extra forward pass per cycle and a small amount of historical state.

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:28.680
The overhead stays modest because verification reuses the same reward model already in the loop.

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:37.420
The quality gain is largest on tasks where local advantage estimates are noisy, such as math reasoning and multi-step tool use.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:45.600
The gain shrinks once models exceed roughly 30 billion parameters and the base optimizer already produces stable improvements.

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:53.920
The two-phase structure also improves cross-seed stability without requiring larger batch sizes or more gradient steps.

00:08:54.520 --> 00:09:06.080
Teams should reach for peep-eye style verification when they observe high variance across random seeds or when wall-clock efficiency matters more than squeezing the last point of benchmark score.

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:15.120
Point a semantic kernel agent at an existing MCP server and let it load skills on demand instead of baking them into the binary.

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:25.020
Add the PIPO verification layer to your current RL post-training run on math or code tasks and compare seed to seed stability.

00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:35.440
Enable GPT Live on an enterprise workspace and test real-time voice workflows that previously required separate transcription plus LLM steps.

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:43.020
Evaluate Cortex AI Gateway's task-scoped tokens if you are already routing agents through snowflake data.

